    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 201722\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1022 AM PDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry and seasonable conditions will give way to increased \n  precipitation chances and a relative cool down on Monday and \n  Tuesday.\n\n* Temperatures will recover during the second half of the week\n  with low precipitation chances returning for our eastern zones\n  as we head into the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Saturday. \n\nDry, mild conditions and seasonable temperatures are on tap for \nsouthern Nevada, northwestern Arizona, and southeastern California \ntoday as we find ourselves under a transient ridge aloft. A low \npressure system will push inland and move through the area on \nMonday and Tuesday, bringing 30 to 60 percent chances of isolated-\nto- scattered showers and thunderstorms to Inyo County, the \nsouthern Great Basin, and the Arizona Strip. There will be a \nslight uptick in precipitable water (PW) with this system, which \nwhen combined with the forcing associated with this low pressure \nsystem, will help to fuel the aforementioned isolated-to-scattered\nstorms. The Mojave Desert will see PWs increase to around 0.8 to \n1.0 inches, while PWs across the southern Great Basin increase to \naround 0.4 to 0.7 inches. In addition to isolated showers and \nthunderstorms, this system will also bring slightly cooler \ntemperatures to the area. High temperatures will cool around 3 to \n6 degrees from today to Tuesday, with overnight low temperatures \ndropping around 2 to 4 degrees. \n\nAfter this low pressure system clears out on Wednesday we will see \n500 mb heights begin to rise as southwesterly flow sets up across \nthe area. These rising heights will allow for temperatures to \ngradually increase through the end of the week, while the \nsouthwesterly flow helps dry things back out. By Friday, \ntemperatures across the Mojave Desert will top out in the mid-90s \nto lower-100s, while temperatures across the southern Great Basin \npeak in the lower-80s to lower-90s. Ensemble guidance continues to\nhint towards an uptick in moisture for the southern and eastern \nportions of our forecast area on Friday and Saturday. Currently, \nthe best chances of precipitation associated with this moisture \nare around 10 to 20 percent in Mohave County on Saturday. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds\nwill follow typical daily directional patterns through Monday \nmorning, with speeds expected to remain around 8KT or less. Early \nMonday afternoon, winds will shift to the southeast, increasing to\naround 10KT with sporadic higher gusts to 15-18KT expected, \nespecially during the mid-afternoon. VFR conditions will prevail \nwith increasing mid-level and high clouds with bases at or above \n15kft.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds through the next\n24 hours will generally follow typical daily directional \npatterns, with speeds around 5-10KT. The primary exception will be\nthe northern Owens Valley, including KBIH, where up-valley winds \nwill become gusty this afternoon, with gusts to 20-25KT expected \nat times through early evening. VFR conditions will prevail with \nincreasing clouds with bases at or above 15kft.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman \nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
